ad.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/ae/PM_100742_E_Cadiz.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled"},{"n":"Gran Teatro Falla","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":36.5337,"lo":-6.3024,"d":1287,"ds":"The Gran Teatro Falla is a theater in the city of Cádiz, Andalusia, Spain. It is located in the Plaza Fragela, facing the Casa de las Viudas and adjacent to the Faculty of Medicine of the University of Cádiz. Construction of the theater began in 1884, following plans by architect Adolfo Morales de los Ríos.","wpu":"Gran Teatro Falla","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cb/Gran_Teatro_Falla.jpg/960px-Gran_Teatro_Falla.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Jardín Botánico (Cádiz)","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":36.5347,"lo":-6.3036,"d":1395,"ds":"The Jardín Botánico de Cádiz was an eighteenth-century example of a botanical garden for the study of medicine located in Cádiz, Spain. It was founded by Pedro Virgili, director of the Royal College of Surgeons of the Navy (now part of the University of Cádiz). Virgili set about acquiring the site for the garden on April 22, 1749, shortly after the foundation of the Royal College.","wpu":"Jardín Botánico (Cádiz)","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/31/Vistas_desde_Hospital_Real_de_C%C3%A1diz_-IMG_20210702_120934.jpg/960px-Vistas_desde_Hospital_Real_de_C%C3%A1diz_-IMG_20210702_120934.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"La Caleta, Spain","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":36.530278,"lo":-6.305833,"d":1646,"ds":"La Caleta is a beach located in the historical center of the city of Cádiz, Spain. It is a natural harbor by which Phoenicians, Carthaginians and Romans penetrated historically. It is the smallest beach in the city, and is isolated from the others.","wpu":"La Caleta, Spain","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/80/Cadiz_playa_caleta.jpg/960px-Cadiz_playa_caleta.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Castle of Santa Catalina (Cádiz)","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":36.532725,"lo":-6.30805,"d":1796,"ds":"The Castle of Santa Catalina (Spanish: Baluarte de Santa catalina y Castillo) is a castle located in Cádiz, Spain. It was declared Bien de Interés Cultural in 1993.","wpu":"Castle of Santa Catalina (Cádiz)","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c4/Castillo_de_Santa_Catalina%2C_C%C3%A1diz.jpg/960px-Castillo_de_Santa_Catalina%2C_C%C3%A1diz.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Castle of San Sebastián","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":36.528106,"lo":-6.315614,"d":2552,"ds":"The Castle of San Sebastián (Spanish: Castillo de San Sebastián) is a fortress located in Cádiz, Spain, at the end of La Caleta beach on a small island separated from the main city. It was declared Bien de Interés Cultural in 1993.","wpu":"Castle of San Sebastián","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fd/Castillo_de_San_Sebasti%C3%A1n_C%C3%A1diz_3.jpg/960px-Castillo_de_San_Sebasti%C3%A1n_C%C3%A1diz_3.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"La Constitución de 1812 Bridge","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":36.524722,"lo":-6.256944,"d":2960,"ds":"The Constitution of 1812 Bridge, also known as La Pepa Bridge (Spanish: El puente de la Constitución de 1812 or Puente de La Pepa), is a bridge across the Bay of Cádiz, linking Cádiz with Puerto Real in mainland Spain. It was built in 2015. Cádiz's first bridge, the Carranza bridge, was inaugurated in 1969, and is now crossed by some 40,000 vehicles per day.","wpu":"La Constitución de 1812 Bridge","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5c/Puente_de_la_Constituci%C3%B3n_de_1812%2C_C%C3%A1diz%2C_Espa%C3%B1a%2C_2015-12-08%2C_DD_21-24_HDR.JPG/960px-Puente_de_la_Constituci%C3%B3n_de_1812%2C_C%C3%A1diz%2C_Espa%C3%B1a%2C_2015-12-08%2C_DD_21-24_HDR.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"José León de Carranza Bridge","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":36.499722,"lo":-6.254722,"d":4834,"ds":"José León de Carranza Bridge (also referred to as the Carranza Bridge) is a 1400 m steel bridge that connects the city of Cádiz to the mainland. The bridge goes over the Bay of Cádiz. It forms part of the national network of roads with the number N-443.","wpu":"José León de Carranza Bridge","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/71/Puente_Carranza_abierto_%282%29.JPG/960px-Puente_Carranza_abierto_%282%29.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Isla del Trocadero","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":36.516667,"lo":-6.216667,"d":6659,"ds":"Isla del Trocadero (lit. 'trader's island') is an island in the Bay of Cádiz, in Andalusia, Spain.","wpu":"Isla del Trocadero","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/33/TrocaderoCarranza.jpg/960px-TrocaderoCarranza.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Bay of Cádiz","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":36.494167,"lo":-6.216944,"d":7742,"ds":"The Bay of Cádiz is a body of water in the province of Cádiz, Spain, adjacent to the southwestern coast of the Iberian Peninsula. The Bay of Cádiz adjoins the Gulf of Cádiz, a larger body of water which is in the same area but further offshore.","wpu":"Bay of Cádiz","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bf/%28Bah%C3%ADa_de_C%C3%A1diz%29_Seville%2C_Spain_%2849104522676%29_%28cropped%29.jpg/960px-%28Bah%C3%ADa_de_C%C3%A1diz%29_Seville%2C_Spain_%2849104522676%29_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Plaza de toros de El Puerto","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":36.596944,"lo":-6.232778,"d":8562,"ds":"The Real Plaza de Toros de El Puerto de Santa María is a bullring in El Puerto de Santa María, Spain. Dating from the 19th century, it has an eclectic architectural style and was completed in 1880. It is the work of Manuel Portillo de Avila y Herrera.","wpu":"Plaza de toros de El Puerto","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e0/Plaza_de_Toros_de_El_Puerto_de_Santa_Mar%C3%ADa_1.JPG/960px-Plaza_de_Toros_de_El_Puerto_de_Santa_Mar%C3%ADa_1.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Castle of San Marcos (El Puerto de Santa María)","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":36.5964,"lo":-6.226642,"d":8841,"ds":"Castle of San Marcos (also Castillo de Alfonso X El Sabio) is a medieval castle located in El Puerto de Santa María, Cádiz, Spain. The castle was erected as a fortified church by King Alfonso X of Castile. It was built on the site of a mosque of which the wall of the qibla survives.","wpu":"Castle of San Marcos (El Puerto de Santa María)","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7d/Castillo_de_San_Marcos%2C_El_Puerto_de_Santa_Mar%C3%ADa%2C_Espa%C3%B1a%2C_2015-12-08%2C_DD_16.JPG/960px-Castillo_de_San_Marcos%2C_El_Puerto_de_Santa_Mar%C3%ADa%2C_Espa%C3%B1a%2C_2015-12-08%2C_DD_16.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Priory Church, El Puerto de Santa María","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":36.599822,"lo":-6.228878,"d":9024,"ds":"The Priory Church (Spanish: Iglesia Mayor Prioral) is a minor basilica church in El Puerto de Santa María, province of Cádiz, Spain. It is documented from 1486 when the building was under construction. It was damaged by an earthquake in the 17th century and was partly rebuilt in the Baroque style.","wpu":"Priory Church, El Puerto de Santa María","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/72/Iglesia_Mayor_Prioral%2C_El_Puerto_de_Santa_Mar%C3%ADa%2C_Espa%C3%B1a%2C_2015-12-08%2C_DD_13.JPG/960px-Iglesia_Mayor_Prioral%2C_El_Puerto_de_Santa_Mar%C3%ADa%2C_Espa%C3%B1a%2C_2015-12-08%2C_DD_13.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"La Cachucha beach","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":36.525556,"lo":-6.185833,"d":9176,"ds":"La Cachucha beach (from Spanish Playa de la Cachucha) is a beach located in Puerto Real, Spain. It's 500 meters long with golden and fin-grain sand. Nowadays the beach needs periodics sand nourishments because natural conditions were modified.","wpu":"La Cachucha beach","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6b/PuertoReal.jpg/960px-PuertoReal.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"}],"about":{"ds":"The Guadalquivir (, also UK: , US: , Spanish: [ɡwaðalkiˈβiɾ]) is the fifth-longest river in the Iberian Peninsula and the second-longest river with its entire length in Spain. The Guadalquivir is the only major navigable river in Spain. Currently it is navigable from Seville to the Gulf of Cádiz, but in Roman times it was both navigable from as far inland as Córdoba and opened into a shallow, yet wide natural harbor at sea.","wpu":"Guadalquivir","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/45/Guadalquivir1.jpg/960px-Guadalquivir1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},"wk":"2026-10-18"}